insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 1, 'The tier scores every inversion against the well''s log after a high cut. What high cut does the course state for that log?', '["50 Hz.", "8 Hz, the stated low-frequency cut that the low-frequency model is smoothed to before it is carried between wells.", "60 Hz, the upper edge of the stated coloured band that the coloured operator acts inside.", "25 Hz, the peak frequency of the stated zero-phase Ricker used by the forward model."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The stated settings print a high cut of 50 Hz for the log a result is scored against. 8 Hz is the LFM cut, 60 Hz the top of the coloured band of 8 to 60 Hz, and 25 Hz the Ricker.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 2, 'The inversion window on the well traces runs from 1100 to 1500 ms, 101 samples every 4.000000 ms. How many of those samples carry a log at Ekene-4?', '["101, because each well''s logs go to two-way time on its checkshots and fill the whole stated window.", "26, from 1284.000000 to 1384.000000 ms.", "27, as at every pack well.", "21, the Ricker''s samples."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The pack''s logs cover the Ekene Sand and the shale above it, so only part of the window carries a log: 26 samples at Ekene-4 and 27 at the other three wells. 21 is the wavelet''s sample count.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 3, 'In the engine''s forwardPoststack, what is the unknown, and how does it reach the seismic trace?', '["AI itself, convolved with the wavelet as d = C m with no difference step.", "A sparse reflectivity r, integrated to ln(AI) after the trace is matched.", "m = ln(AI) on the trace clock, through d = C D m: a centred difference, then a centred convolution.", "Vp alone, with density held at the cap rock''s mean."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The unknown is m = ln(AI); D is the centred first difference and C the centred convolution, so d = C D m. Half the difference of ln(AI) is close to the reflection coefficient for small contrasts, which is why the model is written in ln(AI). A sparse reflectivity belongs to the sparse-spike solver.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 4, 'How does the forward model define D, its difference step, at a sample i and at the two ends of the trace?', '["(m_(i+1) - m_i) / (m_(i+1) + m_i), the exact normal-incidence reflection coefficient at each sample.", "(m_(i+1) - m_i), held at the ends.", "(m_(i+1) - m_(i-1)) / 2, ends from the LFM.", "(m_(i+1) - m_(i-1)) / 2, zero at the first and last sample."]'::jsonb, 3, 'D is the centred first difference, (D m)_i = (m_(i+1) - m_(i-1)) / 2, zero at the first and last sample. The one-sided step is the form the blocky term penalises, and nothing in D reads the LFM.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 5, 'The inversion explorer runs the dot-product test on two seeded vectors of 101 samples, mulberry32 seed 42. What does it print for the centred convolution C with the scaled wavelet?', '["-0.753789 both ways, with no difference.", "38.991017 both ways, a difference of 0.000000: the adjoint passes.", "38.991017 one way and -0.753789 the other, so the transpose is wrong and the solver solves a different problem.", "A correlation of 0.966050 between the two products."]'::jsonb, 1, 'For any x and y, (C x) . y equals x . (C^T y) when the adjoint is right; for C the test prints 38.991017 both ways and a difference of 0.000000. -0.753789 is the pair for the centred difference D, and 0.966050 is the tie at Ekene-1.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 6, 'At Ekene-1 the ln(AI) log, through the scaled wavelet, is set against the full-stack trace at the well. How closely do they agree over the logged samples?', '["They correlate at 0.966050 over 27 samples.", "They correlate at 0.852881 over 27 samples, the agreement the forward model reaches once the solver has run its 60 iterations at the well.", "At 0.966050 over all 101 samples.", "At 0.931237 over 27 samples."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Over the logged samples the synthetic and the trace correlate at 0.966050 on 27 samples. The log is held at its first and last values beyond the logged samples, and the score counts the logged samples alone.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 7, 'In the Ekene-1 table round the top of the Ekene Sand, what do the synthetic and the full-stack trace print at 1296.000000 ms?', '["Synthetic 344.213395, trace 341.516846, the closest pair in the table.", "Synthetic -267.168500, trace -268.438965, a trough that the trace reproduces.", "Synthetic 13.435099, trace -8.919362.", "Synthetic 137.874591, trace 258.968262."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 1296.000000 ms the synthetic is 137.874591 and the trace 258.968262, so the trace is larger there. The close pair 344.213395 and 341.516846 is at 1288.000000 ms, -267.168500 against -268.438965 is at 1304.000000 ms, and 13.435099 against -8.919362 is the row at 1280.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 8, 'Why does every absolute inversion in this tier need a low-frequency model?', '["The LFM supplies the frequencies above the 25 Hz Ricker that the trace cannot carry.", "The scale of 769.984107 is read off the LFM, so without one the wavelet keeps its unit peak.", "The band-limited trace says how impedance changes and nothing of its level; the wells supply what lies below about 8 Hz.", "The adjoint test of 38.991017 is computed on the LFM before any trace is used."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The seismic band holds no trend: a band-limited trace says how impedance changes and says nothing of its level, and the LFM supplies what the seismic lacks below about 8 Hz, from the wells. The scale is set at the wells by least squares, and the adjoint test runs on seeded vectors.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 9, 'lfm.js low-passes each well''s ln(AI) by a moving average whose half window is round(1000 / (cut x dt) / 2) samples. With the stated cut and the 4.000000 ms clock, what half window does it use?', '["8 samples, one for each hertz of the stated cut, so the half window follows the cut directly.", "16 samples.", "27 samples, the whole logged length at Ekene-1, so that each well is smoothed to one value.", "21 samples, the length of the stated Ricker, so the smoothing matches the wavelet''s span."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At a cut of 8 Hz on the 4.000000 ms clock the half window is 16 samples (lowFrequencyModel). 27 is the logged count at Ekene-1 and 21 the Ricker''s samples.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 10, 'With Ekene-1 left out, the LFM at Ekene-1 is carried from the other three wells by inverse distance weighting with power 2. What share does Ekene-4 take?', '["0.477419, the largest share, because Ekene-4 is the farthest well and inverse distance weighting favours the most distant control point.", "0.377419, as Ekene-3.", "0.145161 to Ekene-2 at 1209.338662 m, since the nearest well is the one an inverse square discounts most.", "0.145161, at 2193.171220 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each weight is the inverse square of the distance, and a share is a weight over the sum of the three: Ekene-2 at 1209.338662 m takes 0.477419, Ekene-3 at 1360.147051 m takes 0.377419, and Ekene-4 at 2193.171220 m takes 0.145161, the smallest.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 11, 'lfm.js maps the Ekene-1 trace''s times onto Ekene-2''s horizons, the top and base of the Ekene Sand. Where does the Ekene-1 time 1299.913186 ms land?', '["At 1318.075904 ms, the same proportional position in Ekene-2''s sand.", "At 1305.746335 ms, Ekene-2''s top Ekene Sand horizon.", "At 1330.405474 ms, Ekene-2''s base Ekene Sand horizon.", "At 1338.405474 ms, keeping its offset from the base horizon."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Between two horizons a sample keeps its proportional position (mapToWellTime), so 1299.913186 ms at Ekene-1 lands at 1318.075904 ms at Ekene-2. 1305.746335 and 1330.405474 are Ekene-2''s horizons, and the offset rule applies only above the first horizon and below the last.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 12, 'The RMS AI error of the LFM alone is printed with each well left out of its own LFM and kept in. What happens at Ekene-2 when the well is kept in?', '["Its error falls, from 11.272271 to 10.450743 percent, as it does at every pack well when the well''s own log joins its LFM.", "It is unchanged at 10.450743 percent.", "Its error rises, from 10.450743 to 11.272271 percent.", "It falls to 7.817522 percent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Left out, Ekene-2''s LFM-only error is 10.450743 percent; kept in it is 11.272271. At Ekene-1, Ekene-3 and Ekene-4 keeping the well lowers its error, and 7.817522 percent is Ekene-3 kept in, so a well inside its own LFM is no guarantee of a closer match.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 13, 'The 25 Hz Ricker, peak amplitude 1.000000, is scaled against the full stack at the four pack wells. What scale does the engine return, and on how many samples?', '["769.984107 per well, fitted separately at each of the four wells and then averaged into the one field figure.", "1.000000, the unit peak.", "769.984107, over 101 samples, the whole inversion window at every one of the four wells pooled.", "769.984107, over 19 samples."]'::jsonb, 3, 'waveletScale finds the one number s that makes s times the forward model of the logs match the seismic best, pooled over every well sample whose wavelet span the log covers: 769.984107 over 19 samples. The unit Ricker''s peak of 1.000000 becomes a scaled peak of 769.984107.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 14, 'A case''s wells overlap fewer than ten usable samples of seismic. What does the wavelet scale engine do?', '["It refuses, in the engine''s own words: \"The wells overlap too little seismic to scale the wavelet.\"", "It returns a scale of 1.000000, the stated wavelet''s unit peak.", "It returns a NaN scale, a result with no refusal.", "It refuses with \"A spectrum needs at least 8 samples.\""]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine refuses to scale when the wells overlap fewer than ten usable samples of seismic: "The wells overlap too little seismic to scale the wavelet." The Ekene pack clears that rule with 19 samples. The spectrum message belongs to amplitudeSpectrum.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-forward-model', 15, 'The LFM is built from wells whose logs cover only part of the window. What value does lfm.js give a sample that no well covers?', '["A refusal naming the sample, since the engine declines to build a model where no well carries a log in the window.", "The nearest covered value.", "Zero in ln(AI).", "A value extrapolated along the trend of the last logged samples, continued to the edge of the window."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Samples no well covers hold the nearest covered value. The logs cover the Ekene Sand and the shale above it, 27 or 26 of the 101 samples at each well, so outside them that stated rule decides the LFM, and a study states the wells and the horizons it used.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 1, 'What does the engine''s modelBased minimise, and what does the weight eps do?', '["1/2 ||C r - d||^2 + lambda ||r||_1 by FISTA; eps sets how sparse r is.", "||C D m - d||^2 alone by CGLS; eps is the step length of each iteration.", "||C D m - d||^2 + eps^2 ||m - m0||^2 by CGLS from the LFM m0; eps pulls the result towards the LFM.", "||m - m0||^2 alone; eps scales the wavelet before the solver runs."]'::jsonb, 2, 'modelBased minimises ||C D m - d||^2 + eps^2 ||m - m0||^2 by conjugate gradients on the normal equations, starting from the LFM, and eps pulls the result towards it; the course states eps 0.050000. The lambda ||r||_1 problem is the sparse-spike solver''s.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 2, 'The blind model-based inversion at Ekene-1 (eps 0.050000) is run at 20 and at 300 iterations. How do the residual RMS and the AI error at the well move between them?', '["The residual falls from 3.419041 to 1.233454 while the AI error rises from 8.300748 to 52.851805 percent.", "Both fall, the residual from 3.419041 to 1.233454 and the AI error from 52.851805 to 8.300748 percent.", "Both rise, since more iterations let the noise in the trace grow in the residual and in the error alike.", "The residual is fixed at 2.121830 and only the AI error moves, from 8.300748 to 52.851805 percent."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The residual keeps falling with more iterations while the error at the well rises, because the solver fits the trace beyond what the stated wavelet and the scale can explain. A smaller residual is no better inversion; 2.121830 is the residual at 60 iterations.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 3, 'Of the four iteration counts the course runs for the blind model-based inversion at Ekene-1, which gives the lowest AI error at the well?', '["60 iterations, the stated count, at 10.557858 percent, since the stated setting is the one tuned to this well.", "300 iterations, at 52.851805 percent, the run with the smallest residual of 1.233454 and so the closest fit.", "120 iterations, at 19.965981 percent, where the correlation and the residual both sit in the middle of the range.", "20 iterations, at 8.300748 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The table prints AI errors of 8.300748, 10.557858, 19.965981 and 52.851805 percent at 20, 60, 120 and 300 iterations. Here the iteration count is also the regularisation, and the smallest residual belongs to the worst error.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 4, 'At 300 iterations the weight eps is raised from 0.050000 to 5.000000. What does the blind score at Ekene-1 show?', '["Correlation 0.091593 and AI error 52.851805 percent, unchanged by the weight.", "Correlation 0.732457 and AI error 13.421322 percent, with a larger residual of 1.769102.", "A smaller residual of 1.233454 with the correlation at 0.219646, since a heavier weight fits the trace more closely.", "Correlation 0.852881 and AI error 10.557858 percent, as at 60 iterations."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 300 iterations eps 5.000000 gives a correlation of 0.732457, an AI error of 13.421322 percent and a residual of 1.769102, against 0.091593, 52.851805 and 1.233454 at eps 0.050000: a heavier weight holds the result nearer the LFM at the cost of a larger residual.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 5, 'In the blind model-based inversion at Ekene-1 (eps 0.050000, 60 iterations), how does the inverted AI at 1296.000000 ms compare with the log after the high cut?', '["It is 5168379.152075, the LFM''s value, since the LFM fixes the level inside the sand.", "It is 7582152.255748, above the log''s 6044332.734122.", "It is 5312165.284155, just below the log, as the solver stops short of the step.", "It is 4686769.690526, below the log, because eps pulls it down to the LFM."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 1296.000000 ms the log after the high cut is 6044332.734122, the LFM 5168379.152075 and the inverted AI 7582152.255748, above both. 5312165.284155 and 4686769.690526 are the inverted values at 1288.000000 and 1280.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 6, 'QI Studio''s starting sparse-spike lambda is 0.002000. How many of the 101 reflectivity samples in the Ekene-1 trace are non-zero, and what does that mean?', '["96 at 60 iterations and 61 at 400, since any positive lambda removes the weakest spikes at once.", "36 at 60 iterations and 27 at 400, the sparse earth the method is named for.", "None: the engine refuses a lambda far below the trace''s largest amplitude of 399.814697.", "101 at 60 and at 400 iterations: on traces of this amplitude that lambda imposes no sparsity."]'::jsonb, 3, 'lambda is weighed against a misfit in trace units, and on a trace whose largest absolute amplitude is 399.814697, lambda 0.002000 leaves 101 of 101 samples non-zero. 96 and 61 belong to lambda 200.000000, and 36 and 27 to lambda 5000.000000. The stated value silently decides that no sparsity is imposed.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 7, 'Three methods are run blind at Ekene-1 and scored against the log after the high cut over 27 samples. How do they rank?', '["Sparse-spike leads at 0.873414 and 8.356661 percent; blocky trails at 0.751173 and 13.130119 percent.", "Blocky leads at 0.873414 and 8.356661 percent, since few large steps suit a sand under a shale.", "Model-based leads at 0.852881 and 10.557858 percent; sparse-spike trails at 0.751173.", "All three tie at 0.852881, as each shares the same LFM, wavelet and scale at the well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The table prints model-based 0.852881 and 10.557858, blocky 0.751173 and 13.130119, and sparse-spike 0.873414 and 8.356661 percent. Sparse-spike leads on both measures, at a lambda of 0.002000 that imposes no sparsity.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 8, 'What does coloured inversion need, and what does it return?', '["A scaled wavelet and the LFM, and it returns absolute AI on the trace clock with the level the wells supply.", "The LFM alone, and it returns AI whose level follows the wells and whose shape comes from the stated band.", "No wavelet and no LFM; it returns relative impedance, band-limited, with no level.", "A wavelet per well, and it returns absolute AI with a phase of 0 degrees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Coloured inversion applies one operator to every trace, an amplitude spectrum from a power law f^alpha inside the band of 8 to 60 Hz and a phase of minus 90 degrees. No wavelet and no LFM, so the output is relative impedance with no level.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 9, 'How does the course score coloured inversion at the wells, and why?', '["By the RMS AI error against the log after the 50 Hz high cut, the same measure as the model-based blind well.", "By correlation against the log after the 50 Hz high cut, which keeps the level the operator lacks.", "By the residual RMS in trace units, since no log can be compared with a relative result.", "By correlation alone against the log band-passed between 8 and 50 Hz, since relative impedance has no level to compare."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Coloured inversion is scored by correlation alone against the log band-passed between 8 and 50 Hz, because relative impedance has no level to compare; Ekene-1 scores 0.931237. The absolute inversions are scored against the log after the 50 Hz high cut.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 10, 'The coloured operator uses the mean slope alpha -1.012769 of the four pack wells, whose own slopes run from -0.646563 at Ekene-1 to -1.473109 at Ekene-2. Why do the four slopes differ?', '["Ekene-2''s slope carries the wrong polarity, so its impedance spectrum falls where the other wells'' rise.", "The logs are short, so each slope rests on few frequencies.", "Each well has its own transform length, shorter than 256.", "The band of 8 to 60 Hz clips each well''s spectrum at a different edge."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The pack covers the Ekene Sand and the shale above it, so each log is short and each slope rests on few frequencies; that is why the four slopes differ. The operator uses one transform length, 256, and the slope is fitted over 5 to 80 Hz.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 11, 'In the model-based blind-well table (eps 0.050000, 60 iterations), what does the LFM alone show beside the blind inversion?', '["A negative correlation at every well, and a lower RMS error than the blind inversion at 4 of 4 wells.", "A positive correlation at every well, as the LFM carries both the level and the shape of AI between wells.", "A higher RMS error than the blind inversion at 4 of 4 wells, since an LFM carries no detail of the sand.", "A lower RMS error at 2 of 4 wells only, Ekene-2 and Ekene-3, with a negative correlation at each well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The LFM alone correlates at -0.190352 to -0.345505, negative at every well: it carries the level and none of the shape. Its RMS error is lower than the model-based blind error at 4 of 4 wells, 8.638086 against 10.557858 percent at Ekene-1. The 2 of 4 figure belongs to sparse-spike.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 12, 'Against the LFM-only RMS error, where does the blind sparse-spike inversion have the lower AI error?', '["At all four pack wells, as its blind correlations from 0.778867 to 0.881157 suggest.", "At Ekene-2 and Ekene-3, where its blind correlations are the highest of the four wells.", "At Ekene-1 and Ekene-4.", "At none of the wells, since the LFM alone has the lower error than any inversion at every well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sparse-spike''s blind AI error is 8.356661 percent at Ekene-1 against the LFM''s 8.638086, and 10.144841 at Ekene-4 against 10.307658; the LFM has the lower error at Ekene-2 and Ekene-3. A study quotes both figures.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 13, 'Twenty noisy realisations of the Ekene-1 full stack are each inverted blind. What is the mean relative spread in AI over the logged samples?', '["0.727077, the largest relative spread the table prints, at 1304.000000 ms.", "0.507162, the spread at 1280.000000 ms.", "0.645787, the mean of (Q90 - Q10) / Q50 over the 27 logged samples.", "0.546074, the spread at 1296.000000 ms."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per sample the quantiles across the realisations give (Q90 - Q10) / Q50, and its mean over the 27 logged samples is 0.645787. 0.507162, 0.546074 and 0.727077 are single samples at 1280.000000, 1296.000000 and 1304.000000 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 14, 'In the course''s percentile convention for parameters, what is Q10 of AI across the realisations?', '["The LOW figure, the value 10 percent of realisations fall below.", "The HIGH figure, the value 10 percent of realisations exceed, labelled as volumes are labelled.", "The median of the realisations, read as Q50 once ten seeds have been run from each of the twenty.", "The relative spread, (Q90 - Q10) / Q50, at the sample."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Q10 is the 10th percentile, the value 10 percent of realisations fall below, so it is the LOW figure; at 1288.000000 ms it is 4056484.639171 against Q90 6743113.590982. P-labels are kept for outcomes such as volumes, where P90 is the low figure.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-post-stack-inversion', 15, 'The realisations add seeded Gaussian noise at a signal-to-noise ratio of 4 on the 20 seeds 101 to 120. What may a study claim for the spread they give?', '["The uncertainty of the AI at Ekene-1 from every source, the LFM, the wavelet and the iteration count included.", "A fixed property of the trace, the same on any seeds once at least 20 realisations have been run.", "The spread of the field''s AI between the wells, since each realisation is inverted blind at the well.", "An estimate on stated seeds and a stated noise level; a different set of seeds gives different figures."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The realisations differ in one assumption, the noise, at a signal-to-noise ratio of 4 on seeds 101 to 120, and the mean relative spread of 0.645787 is an estimate on those seeds and that noise level. A different set of seeds gives different figures.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 1, 'In Fatti''s linearisation at the Ekene background Vs/Vp, what do the three coefficients print at normal incidence?', '["G1 0.515546, G2 -0.018761 and G3 -0.006165.", "G1 0.500000, with G2 and G3 both 0.000000: only AI contributes.", "G1 0.000000, with shear and density carrying it.", "G1 and G3 both 0.500000, with G2 zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With G1 = (1 + tan^2 theta) / 2 and both G2 and G3 carrying sin^2 theta or tan^2 theta, the 0 degree row prints G1 0.500000 and G2 and G3 0.000000. 0.515546, -0.018761 and -0.006165 are the 10 degree row.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 2, 'What background Vs/Vp, K, does the course use for the Fatti coefficients on the Ekene pack, and how is it found?', '["0.394396, the Vs / Vp of the Ekene-1 oil leg alone, the zone the inversion is meant to resolve.", "0.500000, the value at which G1 sits at normal incidence, taken as K so the coefficients balance.", "0.666667, the G1 coefficient at the far stack of 30 degrees, read as the background ratio.", "0.394396, the mean of Vs / Vp over every logged sample of the four pack wells in the window."]'::jsonb, 3, 'K is the mean of Vs / Vp over every logged sample of the four pack wells in the window, 0.394396, and it enters G2 and G3 through K^2. 0.500000 and 0.666667 are G1 at 0 and 30 degrees.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 3, 'At the pack''s near angle of 10 degrees, what is G3, the density coefficient?', '["-0.006165.", "-0.018761, the coefficient on dln(SI) at 10 degrees, the second voice of the three.", "-0.029846, the density coefficient at the mid stack of 20 degrees, read one row down.", "0.515546, the coefficient on dln(AI) at 10 degrees, which every stack sees most."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 10 degrees G1 is 0.515546, G2 -0.018761 and G3 -0.006165; G3 at 20 degrees is -0.029846. Density''s coefficient is negative and the smallest of the three in size at every non-zero angle in the table.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 4, 'Between which pair of tabled angles does the size of G3 grow the most, and how far do the pack''s stacks reach?', '["Between 0 and 10 degrees, where density enters; the far stack reaches 40.", "Between 20 and 30 degrees, -0.029846 to -0.088893; the far stack reaches 40.", "Between 30 and 40 degrees, from -0.088893 to -0.223507; the far stack reaches 30 degrees.", "Evenly at each step, G3 being linear in angle; the far stack reaches 30."]'::jsonb, 2, 'G3 runs 0.000000, -0.006165, -0.029846, -0.088893 and -0.223507 at 0 to 40 degrees, and its size grows more between 30 and 40 degrees than between any earlier pair. The far stack here reaches 30 degrees, so G3 grows most beyond the pack''s angles.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 5, 'What does simultaneousInversion solve for, and how is each unknown held?', '["ln(AI) first by CGLS, then ln(SI) and ln(rho) from it by Fatti''s coefficients with no LFM.", "ln(AI), ln(SI) and ln(rho) together by CGLS, each pulled to its own LFM by its own weight.", "AI, Vp/Vs and density in turn, one stack each, with one shared weight on all three.", "Intercept and gradient by a Shuey fit to the stacks, then AI from the intercept."]'::jsonb, 1, 'simultaneousInversion runs pylops'' PrestackLinearModelling with the Fatti linearisation and solves for ln(AI), ln(SI) and ln(rho) together by CGLS, each pulled to its own LFM by its own weight; the course states 0.050000 for each and 60 iterations.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 6, 'Each angle stack takes the same 25 Hz Ricker shape with its own least squares scale (fattiScale). How do the three scales run from near to far?', '["They rise with angle, the far stack''s larger scale making up for the high frequency it has lost.", "They are one figure, 769.984107, since one Ricker shape carries one scale across every stack.", "They match the angle''s G1, rising from 0.515546 at 10 degrees to 0.666667 at 30 degrees.", "They fall: near 1860.598608, mid 1679.662569, far 1305.654435."]'::jsonb, 3, 'fattiScale gives near 1860.598608, mid 1679.662569 and far 1305.654435, each over 19 samples: the scale falls from near to far. 769.984107 is the post-stack scale of the full stack, and the shape is the same Ricker for every stack.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 7, 'The well-wavelet extraction under extractAngleWavelets is run at Ekene-1, on the half differences of the ln(AI) log, with a half length of 15 samples, a 31-sample wavelet against 27 logged samples. What does it say?', '["\"The well reflectivity is flat over the seismic at the well, so there is nothing to extract a wavelet from.\"", "\"The log covers 16 usable samples at the well; a 11-sample wavelet needs at least 22. Shorten the wavelet or use the statistical one.\"", "\"The wells overlap too little seismic to scale the wavelet.\"", "Nothing: it returns a 31-sample wavelet for each of the 3 stacks."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With a half length of 15 the wavelet is longer than the 27 logged samples, so no row of the fit is complete, and the engine''s own words are the flat-reflectivity message; over the four wells it returns no wavelet for 0 of 3 stacks. The 16-sample message comes at a half length of 5.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 8, 'At what half length does extractAngleWavelets return a wavelet for each stack on the pack, and why does the course still state the Ricker shape for the inversions?', '["At 5, an 11-sample wavelet from 16 usable samples per well.", "At 15, a 31-sample wavelet, the engine''s own length.", "At 3, a 7-sample wavelet spanning 24.000000 ms, shorter than the stated Ricker.", "At no length: 0 of 3 stacks at every half length."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a half length of 3 the engine returns a 7-sample wavelet for each of the 3 stacks; it spans 24.000000 ms, shorter than the stated Ricker, so the course states the Ricker shape and scales it per stack. At 5 the engine says an 11-sample wavelet needs at least 22 usable samples.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 9, 'With 7-sample angle wavelets at 30 degrees, Ekene-4 prints a fit correlation of 0.967385. How well does its synthetic match the far stack?', '["At 0.967385, the same figure, since a well''s synthetic against the stack is its fit correlation read a second way.", "At 0.603319, below its fit correlation.", "At 0.927864, the figure Ekene-1''s synthetic reaches against the far stack, which every well shares at that angle.", "At 0.940659, the fit correlation at Ekene-1, as the four wells'' fits are aligned and averaged into one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 30 degrees Ekene-4''s fit correlation is 0.967385 and its synthetic against the stack 0.603319. Ekene-1 prints 0.940659 and 0.927864. A close fit to the well''s own reflectivity is no guarantee of a close match to the stack.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 10, 'Ekene-1 is inverted blind from the near stack only (eps 0.050000 for each parameter, 60 iterations). What happens to Vp/Vs?', '["At 0.470296 and 10.707173 percent, shear carried by G2.", "At the LFMs'' 0.048283 and 11.122749 percent, unchanged.", "It correlates at 0.383124 with an error of 13.660754 percent, the best Vp/Vs the three sets of stacks give.", "It correlates at -0.744719 with an error of 30.824755 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With the near stack only, Vp/Vs scores -0.744719 and 30.824755 percent, worse than the LFMs alone at 0.048283 and 11.122749. 0.383124 is near and mid, and 0.470296 with 10.707173 percent is near, mid and far.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 11, 'From the near stack only, density''s RMS error at Ekene-1 is 0.627310 percent against 0.767482 with the LFMs alone. What does the near stack do to density?', '["It moves density''s shape, correlation -0.087754 to 0.614032, without moving its level far.", "It sets density''s level from the seismic, the error under one percent coming from the near stack.", "It moves density''s level by 14.112317 percent while its correlation stays at the LFMs'' -0.087754.", "Nothing: G3 is 0.000000 at 10 degrees, so density stays exactly at its LFM."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With the near stack, where G3 is tiny at -0.006165, density''s error stays under one percent while its correlation changes from -0.087754 to 0.614032: the near stack moves density''s shape without moving its level far. G3 is 0.000000 only at 0 degrees, and 14.112317 percent is the error with all three stacks.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 12, 'Add the mid and far stacks to the near at Ekene-1, blind. What happens to density?', '["Its correlation rises to 0.863789 and its error falls to 8.956955 percent.", "It holds at 0.614032 and 0.627310 percent, unchanged by the stacks.", "Its correlation turns negative, -0.767957, and its error rises to 14.112317 percent.", "Its error falls to the LFMs'' 0.767482 percent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With near, mid and far, density scores -0.767957 and 14.112317 percent at Ekene-1, and with near and mid -0.707948 and 12.184029: density is the least reliable of the three parameters. 0.863789 and 8.956955 are the AI scores with all three stacks.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 13, 'How does AI''s correlation at Ekene-1 change across the three sets of stacks, near, near and mid, and near, mid and far?', '["It rises steadily as stacks join, from 0.858677 to 0.865225, with the far stack lifting it most of all.", "It hardly changes: 0.858677, 0.865225 and 0.863789.", "It falls with each stack added, from 0.863789 to 0.858677, since the far stacks carry less of G1 than the near stack.", "It goes negative with the far stack, to -0.190352."]'::jsonb, 1, 'AI scores 0.858677, 0.865225 and 0.863789 from the three sets of stacks: AI is held by the near angles alone. -0.190352 is the LFMs alone, and 0.865225 is the near and mid figure.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 14, 'Every pack well is inverted blind from the near, mid and far stacks. What sign does the density correlation take across the four wells?', '["Positive at all four wells, from 0.613638 to 0.910855, following the far stack''s synthetic correlations.", "Positive at Ekene-1 and Ekene-3 and negative at Ekene-2 and Ekene-4, the pattern Vp/Vs shows.", "Negative at Ekene-1 only, where the single-well experiment ran, and positive at the other three wells.", "Negative at all four wells."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The density correlations are -0.767957, -0.470987, -0.719301 and -0.413424 at Ekene-1 to Ekene-4. The split by well belongs to Vp/Vs, positive at Ekene-1 and Ekene-3 at 0.470296 and 0.360220. A study quotes the blind figure for each parameter it uses.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-simultaneous-inversion', 15, 'Which of the three parameters does the blind check from near, mid and far stacks support at every pack well?', '["Vp/Vs, with errors from 10.707173 to 15.482932 percent, the smallest of the three at each pack well.", "Density, its error lowest at Ekene-4, 9.115991 percent.", "AI, with correlations from 0.780557 at Ekene-4 to 0.863789 at Ekene-1.", "None, as each scores below the LFMs'' -0.190352."]'::jsonb, 2, 'AI correlates at 0.863789, 0.854299, 0.847660 and 0.780557 at Ekene-1 to Ekene-4; Vp/Vs and density do not correlate well, and each is reported for what it is. Density''s 9.115991 percent at Ekene-4 sits beside a correlation of -0.413424.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 1, 'fitLinearTransform is run on the 644 net sand samples of the Ekene Sand at the four pack wells, both legs, AI (acoustic impedance) against the kit''s PHIT. What does it return?', '["a 0.302982, b -0.017033 per 10^6 kg/(m2 s) and s 0.007175, the same fit on the 644 log samples.", "a 0.363690, b -0.022834 per 10^6 kg/(m2 s), s 0.017980 and R squared 0.323708.", "a 0.363690 with a positive slope b per 10^6 kg/(m2 s), because harder sand carries more porosity.", "a 0.363690, b -0.022834 per 10^6 kg/(m2 s), with s 0.024072, the pooled scatter of the four wells about the line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At log scale on 644 samples the fit is a 0.363690, b -0.022834 per 10^6 kg/(m2 s), s 0.017980, R squared 0.323708. The figures 0.302982, -0.017033 and 0.007175 belong to the seismic-scale fit on 20 upscaled samples, 0.024072 is the pooled left-out RMS, and the slope is negative.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 2, 'At AI 7000000 kg/(m2 s), what is Q90 of the log-scale porosity transform at the 80 percent level?', '["0.203850, the predicted porosity, which is the middle of the band and its Q50.", "0.233493, the high end of the interval at the same AI with the level stated at 90 percent.", "0.238378, the high end of the 80 percent interval read on the row of AI 6500000 kg/(m2 s).", "0.226936, the high end of the 80 percent interval about 0.203850."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At AI 7000000 the prediction is 0.203850 and the 80 percent interval runs from 0.180764 (Q10, the low end) to 0.226936 (Q90, the high end). 0.233493 is the 90 percent high end, and 0.238378 is Q90 at AI 6500000.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 3, 'The interval level is raised from 80 to 90 percent at AI 7000000 kg/(m2 s), every other input unchanged. What does predictWithInterval return?', '["A wider interval, 0.174207 to 0.233493, about the same prediction.", "The same interval, 0.180764 to 0.226936, since the width of the band is set by s and n alone and the level only relabels its ends.", "A narrower interval inside 0.180764 to 0.226936, because a higher level asks for more confidence and so a tighter band about the line.", "A prediction moved up to 0.215267 with the 80 percent width kept, since the level shifts the line toward the upper quantile."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The level enters through Student''s t, so the 90 percent interval at AI 7000000 is 0.174207 to 0.233493, wider than the 80 percent 0.180764 to 0.226936. The prediction stays 0.203850; 0.215267 is the prediction at AI 6500000.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 4, 'predictWithInterval is called at 8500000, beyond the highest of the AI values the fit was built on, 8402495.750400. What comes back?', '["It refuses, with a message that the AI lies outside the calibration range of the transform it was given.", "It returns NaN for the prediction and both ends, the way a result with a gap is shown in a panel.", "It still answers, 0.169599 within 0.146406 to 0.192792, its interval widened only by the distance term.", "It answers 0.169599 and adds an out-of-range penalty to the interval on top of the distance term."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The calibration AI runs from 5693260.530000 to 8402495.750400; at 8500000 the engine still answers, 0.169599 with Q10 0.146406 and Q90 0.192792, and the interval widens only by the distance term: the last row lies outside the calibration range, and the engine still answers.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 5, 'The seismic-scale check gives s 0.007175 log against log and a pooled left-out RMS of 0.024072. What widening s_extra does calibrateProperty return?', '["0.024072, the pooled left-out RMS added outright.", "0.022977, the square root of the RMS squared less s squared.", "0.007175, the log-against-log error, which already carries the error of the inversion.", "0.017980, the residual standard error of the log-scale fit on the 644 samples of both legs."]'::jsonb, 1, 's_extra = sqrt(RMS^2 - s^2) when the RMS exceeds s, so 0.024072 and 0.007175 give 0.022977. The RMS is the input, s the log scatter it is set against, and 0.017980 belongs to the log-scale fit.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 6, 'Why does the left-out check widen the porosity band at all?', '["On 20 upscaled samples Student''s t alone widens it, and s_extra is that rise.", "Each left-out well lies beyond the calibration AI, so s_extra is the distance term.", "The 50 Hz low-pass adds smoothing error to the logs, so s_extra measures the scatter the high cut leaves between the logs.", "The left-out residuals carry the inversion''s error, which a log-against-log fit cannot see."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Porosity at each left-out well is predicted from the blind inverted AI there; those residuals carry the inversion''s error, and their pooled RMS of 0.024072 widens the band by 0.022977. s_extra is set from the RMS and s, so neither t nor the distance term nor the high cut is what it measures.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 7, 'How is each well''s share inside the widened 80 percent band tested, and what does Ekene-1 return?', '["With the other wells'' residuals only; Ekene-1 returns 0.600000 on 5 samples.", "With every well''s residuals pooled, its own included; Ekene-1 returns 1.000000 on 5 samples.", "With the other wells'' residuals; Ekene-1 returns 0.833333.", "With the log-against-log s alone, 0.007175; Ekene-1 returns 0.600000, so the narrow band already covers it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each well''s coverage is tested with the other wells'' residuals only, and Ekene-1 returns 0.600000 on 5 samples. 0.833333 on 6 samples is Ekene-2, and 1.000000 is Ekene-3 and Ekene-4.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 8, 'In the left-out table, Ekene-4 returns its correlation as NaN on 4 samples. What is that?', '["A refusal: calibrateProperty stops on a well with fewer than 5 samples and names it in its message.", "A correlation of 0.000000 written as NaN: the two are unrelated.", "A result: the code returns NaN when either series has no spread over the well''s samples.", "The bias, -0.009593, entered in the wrong column when the prediction at the well has no shape to score."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A NaN correlation is the result the code returns when either series has no spread over the well''s samples. It is no refusal: Ekene-4 still returns an RMS of 0.012741, a bias of -0.009593 and a share of 1.000000, and the NaN is reported as a gap.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 9, 'What do the four left-out biases in the seismic-scale check show?', '["They change sign between wells, so they cancel and leave the pooled RMS of 0.024072 to describe the scatter alone.", "Every one is negative, from -0.024560 at Ekene-1 to -0.009593 at Ekene-4.", "Every one is positive, an overprediction at each well.", "Every one is smaller in size than s, 0.007175."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The printed biases are -0.024560, -0.021598, -0.020246 and -0.009593: one sign at every well. Each is larger in size than s, 0.007175.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 10, 'fitFaciesModel is fitted on AI alone with priors from the samples'' proportions. At AI 6600000 kg/(m2 s), what does faciesPosterior return?', '["Oil sand most likely, 0.673039 to 0.326961.", "Oil sand most likely, 0.672121 to 0.280694.", "Shale most likely, at its prior of 0.796396.", "Brine sand most likely, 0.557911 against oil sand 0.442088."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With the sample-proportion priors at AI 6600000, P(oil sand) is 0.442088 and P(brine sand) 0.557911, so brine sand is called. 0.673039 is oil sand under equal priors, 0.672121 is oil sand at AI 6000000, and 0.796396 is the shale prior, whose posterior here is 0.000000.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 11, 'The same one-attribute model is rerun at AI 6600000 kg/(m2 s) with equal priors. What changes, and why?', '["Oil sand becomes most likely, 0.673039; only the stated prior changed.", "Nothing: the posteriors stay 0.442088 and 0.557911.", "Brine sand firms to 0.883173 from its 0.147012 prior.", "Shale is called, its 0.796396 prior set aside."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With equal priors at AI 6600000 the posteriors are P(shale) 0.000000, P(oil sand) 0.673039, P(brine sand) 0.326961: the call flips from brine sand to oil sand. The prior is a stated choice and it moves the answer; 0.883173 is brine sand at AI 7800000.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 12, 'A second attribute, Vp/Vs, is added to the facies model. At AI 6600000 kg/(m2 s), what does the two-attribute model return at Vp/Vs 1.750000 and at 2.000000?', '["Brine sand 0.557911 at both ratios.", "Oil sand 0.673039; brine sand 0.326961.", "Oil sand 0.997373 at 1.750000; brine sand 1.000000 at 2.000000.", "Brine sand 0.997373; oil sand 1.000000."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At AI 6600000 the two-attribute posteriors are oil sand 0.997373 at Vp/Vs 1.750000 and brine sand 1.000000 at 2.000000, where AI alone split 0.442088 to 0.557911.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 13, 'The confusion matrix of the AI-only facies model has the oil-sand row 3, 49, 127 (shale, oil sand, brine sand). What does it show, beside a share correct of 0.934872?', '["160 of the 179 are called oil sand.", "Most of the 179 are called shale.", "About 0.934872 of the 179 are called oil sand.", "Most of the 179 oil-sand samples, 127, are called brine sand."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On AI alone 49 of 179 oil-sand samples are called oil sand and 127 brine sand. Shale is called shale 2469 times of 2519 inside the same share correct of 0.934872. 160 of 179 is the two-attribute row.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 14, 'Adding Vp/Vs raises the share correct from 0.934872 to 0.973443. What happens to the shale row of the confusion matrix?', '["It improves: 2519 of 2519 are called shale.", "It worsens: 2464 correct from 2469, with 36 called brine sand from 5.", "It is unchanged at 2469 correct, since AI alone already separates shale and Vp/Vs only moves the two sands.", "It worsens: 45 called oil sand, from 19."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Shale is called shale 2469 times on AI alone and 2464 times with Vp/Vs, and shale called brine sand rises from 5 to 36. 45 and 19 run the other way: AI alone called 45 shale samples oil sand, the two attributes 19.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-properties-from-impedance', 15, 'What kind of test are the two confusion matrices, and how does QI Studio label the classes oil sand and brine sand?', '["Each is on the model''s own samples; a class named for a fluid is labelled fluid_hypothesis.", "Each is a blind test on left-out wells; oil sand and brine sand are labelled calibrated_prediction.", "Each is on the model''s own samples; every class, shale included, is labelled fluid_hypothesis.", "Each is at seismic scale on blind inverted AI; oil sand is a measured fluid."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both matrices are of each model on its own samples, at log scale. A facies named for a fluid is a hypothesis about the pore fluid: QI Studio labels such a class fluid_hypothesis and every other class, shale here, calibrated_prediction.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 1, 'spillAnalysis runs on the Ekene Sand top grid with no seed and no contact. What column from crest to spill does it return?', '["0.643935 ft, the scatter of the downdip edge of designed anomaly A about one contour.", "6.992237 ft, the edge scatter of designed anomaly B, the anomaly cut at the middle column.", "2.380249 ft, from a crest of -5051.568570 to a spill of -5053.948819 ft.", "20 ft, the level below the spill that bounds designed anomaly B on the Ekene grid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The crest is -5051.568570 ft and the spill -5053.948819 ft, a column of 2.380249 ft. 0.643935 and 6.992237 ft are the edge scatters of anomalies A and B, and 20 ft is B''s stated level below the spill.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 2, 'On that grid the spill is flagged as limited by the map edge, and the closure at the wells'' contact of -5118.110236 ft is reported not closed. What may a study say of the closure figures?', '["They are the map''s limit: six wells grid too little of the structure to close the trap.", "They measure the trap, since the flood found a spill 2.380249 ft below the crest.", "The trap is breached, since oil seen at the wells sits below a spill the map measured.", "They hold at the wells'' contact, since a contact below the spill closes the trap on the map."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The map spills at the edge of its own mapped area, 2.380249 ft below the crest and above the contact the wells see; the engine flags the edge, and a closure figure from this grid is the map''s limit and no measurement of the trap. A contact at or above the spill leaves the closure closed, so -5118.110236 ft leaves it open.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 3, 'How does spillAnalysis find the spill elevation?', '["A priority flood from the crest expands the lowest frontier node first, and the spill is the first node it reaches beside a null.", "It steps from the crest to the lowest of the eight neighbours of each node, and the spill is the elevation where that walk stops.", "It takes the deepest node of the mapped area as the spill and the shallowest as the crest, and the column is their difference.", "A priority flood from the crest expands the highest frontier node; the spill is the running minimum when it first reaches the mapped edge."]'::jsonb, 3, 'From the crest, the highest mapped node unless a seed is stated, a priority flood always expands the highest frontier node; the spill elevation is the running minimum when the flood first reaches the edge of the mapped area. Connectivity is four-neighbour.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 4, 'A depth grid has a gap of null nodes inside the mapped area. How does the closure engine treat a node beside that gap?', '["As an interior node: the flood bridges the gap by eight-neighbour steps.", "As the mapped edge, the same as a node on the edge of the grid.", "As a refusal: the engine stops and asks for a grid with no nulls inside it.", "As the crest, since a node beside a null is the start the flood takes first."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mapped edge is any grid-edge node or any node beside a null, so the flood can spill into a gap. Connectivity is four-neighbour, and the crest is the highest mapped node unless a seed is stated.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 5, 'What does the closure engine return at the spill of the Ekene Sand top grid, beside the column?', '["201 nodes, the mapped part of the grid, and an area of 1184030.145838 ft2 at the spill.", "11 nodes, an area of 1184030.145838 ft2 and a gross rock volume of 1519731.367693 ft3.", "11 nodes and a gross rock volume of 1184030.145838 ft3, the printed area read in cubic feet.", "11 nodes and an oil volume in barrels, turned from the gross rock volume of 1519731.367693 ft3."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the spill the closure holds 11 nodes, area 1184030.145838 ft2, gross rock volume 1519731.367693 ft3. 201 is the count of mapped nodes on the 25 by 20 grid, and closure returns gross rock volume only, no volume in barrels.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 6, 'A prospect is picked at the mapped node nearest the grid''s centre, column 12, row 9, at -5057.417979 ft. Where does climbToCrest end?', '["At node 310, -5051.568570 ft, the same crest as the highest mapped node.", "At the spill, -5053.948819 ft, where the climb meets the edge of the mapped area.", "At -5057.417979 ft: the climb needs a stated seed to step.", "At a local high below the crest, since the climb steps only to its four neighbours."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The climb starts from the nearest mapped node and steps to the highest of its eight neighbours until none is higher; from column 12, row 9 it ends at node 310, -5051.568570 ft, the highest mapped node, so it reaches the same crest.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 7, 'Designed teaching anomaly A, every mapped node shallower than the spill, returns conformance 0.729467. How does the assessment read it?', '["It sits between 0.3 and 0.7.", "It cuts across, short of 1.", "It fits: 0.729467 is at or above 0.7.", "It fits perfectly, since its share inside the closure, 1.000000, is the conformance the table reads."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The decision table counts conformance 0.7 and above as fitting and under 0.3 as cutting across. A returns 0.729467, so it fits; 1.000000 is its share inside the closure, a separate output.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 8, 'Why does a clean contour, anomaly A with an edge scatter of only 0.643935 ft, still score well below a conformance of 1.000000 on this grid?', '["Conformance is 1 - share inside the closure, so A''s 1.000000 share costs it the difference.", "Conformance is the scatter divided by 20 ft, the stated level below the spill used for anomaly B.", "Conformance is read against the wells'' contact at -5118.110236 ft, far below A''s edge.", "Conformance is 1 - scatter / relief, and the relief is the 2.380249 ft column of a map that spills at its edge."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Conformance = 1 - scatter / relief, clipped to 0 to 1, with the relief the closure''s from crest to spill: 2.380249 ft here, the column of a grid that spills at its own edge. A''s 0.643935 ft scatter against that relief gives 0.729467.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 9, 'Anomaly A''s implied contact is -5053.959008 ft and the spill is -5053.948819 ft. What sets A''s edge points?', '["Each is an anomaly node beside a lower node outside it, read at their midpoint.", "Each is the spill itself, so the contact repeats -5053.948819 ft.", "Each is the deepest anomaly node, so the implied contact is A''s lowest mapped depth.", "Each is the wells'' contact read at that node, -5118.110236 ft, carried onto the grid."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine reads the downdip edge as an anomaly node beside a lower node outside it, at their midpoint. A''s edge implies -5053.959008 ft, just below the spill of -5053.948819 ft, and far from the wells'' -5118.110236 ft.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 10, 'Designed anomaly B, cut at the grid''s middle column, has an edge scatter of 6.992237 ft. What does anomalyConformance return for it?', '["A refusal, since B''s scatter is larger than the closure''s whole relief and leaves no conformance.", "Conformance 0.152778, its share inside the closure read as its conformance.", "Conformance 0.000000, share inside 0.152778, and an implied contact of -5068.442175 ft that the engine still returns.", "Conformance 0.729467 and share 1.000000, since B holds every node of A inside it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With a scatter of 6.992237 ft against a relief of 2.380249 ft, 1 - scatter / relief falls below zero and is clipped to 0.000000. B shares 0.152778 of its 72 nodes with the closure; its implied contact of -5068.442175 ft is still returned, and under 0.3 the edge cuts across.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 11, 'A prospect lists a full-stack RMS amplitude, an impedance from the same full stack and a far-stack brightening. What does evidenceIndependence return?', '["3 supporting items and 3 independent sources, one for each attribute in the list.", "1 independent source, since every attribute is read from one seismic survey.", "2 supporting items, the impedance being dropped as a copy of the RMS amplitude.", "3 supporting items and 2 independent sources, with full_stack the shared source."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Attributes computed from one seismic response are one piece of evidence: the RMS amplitude and the impedance share full_stack, and the far-stack brightening is a second source. 3 supporting items, 2 independent sources.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 12, 'The first row of the decision table matures a prospect. With one independent source in place of two, every other input the same, what does assessProspect return?', '["Mature, with support moved down to neutral.", "Investigate, with the seismic support still supports.", "Retain, with neutral support, as for a missing feasibility verdict.", "Downgrade, with support against, since one source is evidence against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Maturing needs two independent supporting sources. The same case with one returns investigate and supports: the recommendation drops while the support holds.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 13, 'No anomaly is seen on a prospect where the feasibility study says the case would be visible. What does the assessment return?', '["Downgrade, with seismic support against.", "Retain, with neutral support, since absence of an anomaly says nothing either way.", "Investigate, with neutral support, as for a competing explanation judged likely.", "Mature, with support against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The absence of an anomaly counts against a prospect only where the feasibility study says the case would be visible: no anomaly, feasible, returns downgrade and against. With feasibility not stated it returns retain and neutral.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 14, 'The first row of the decision table matures a prospect: conformance 0.83, inside 0.9, two independent sources, feasible. A competing explanation (tuning, a lithology change or a processing artefact) is now judged likely, every other input the same. What does the assessment return?', '["Mature, with support kept at supports.", "Downgrade, with support set at against.", "Investigate, with neutral seismic support.", "Retain, with neutral support, the anomaly set aside as an artefact."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A competing explanation is an input with a status of open, ruled out or likely; one judged likely holds the prospect at investigate, and the decision table''s last row returns neutral support.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-prospects', 15, 'What thresholds does the assessment''s decision table state?', '["Conformance 0.3 and above fits, under 0.7 cuts across; three supporting items to mature.", "Conformance 0.7 and above fits, under 0.3 cuts across; two independent supporting sources to mature.", "Conformance 0.729467 and above fits, the score of anomaly A; one independent source to mature.", "Share inside the closure 0.7 and above fits, under 0.3 cuts across; two sources to investigate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The decision table states conformance 0.7 and above fits and under 0.3 cuts across, and two independent supporting sources to mature. 0.729467 is anomaly A''s score, and the thresholds are on conformance.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 1, 'What does a QI study never set?', '["The seismic support word, which the risk team writes from the maps.", "The geological chance of success, which the risk team weighs.", "The recommendation, which the risk team derives from the chance it sets.", "The porosity band, which the risk team widens from its own priors."]'::jsonb, 1, 'QI reports what the seismic says, supports, neutral or against, for the risk team to weigh; the geological chance of success is never set by QI. The support word and the recommendation are QI''s own outputs, and the band is widened by the left-out RMS of 0.024072.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 2, 'What does prospectAssessment.js return for a prospect?', '["A recommendation, a seismic support and a probability of success from 0 to 1.", "A probability of success alone, set from conformance and the source count.", "A seismic support alone, the recommendation being left to the risk team.", "A recommendation and a seismic support, and no probability of success."]'::jsonb, 3, 'prospectAssessment.js returns a recommendation (mature, investigate, retain or downgrade) and a seismic support (supports, neutral or against) and no probability of success. The chance stays with the risk team.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 3, 'Why does the QI report state the settings of every product beside its figures?', '["A result without its settings cannot be checked.", "The engines read their settings from the report file when a reviewer runs them again on the same pack.", "The settings fix the geological chance of success, which the risk team reads straight from the report.", "The engines pick their own settings on each run, so the report records which values each run happened to pick."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The handover files are the products and that record of method, settings, blind figures, calibration, prospect limits and issues; a result without its settings cannot be checked. Every setting is a stated input, and no engine reads a file or sets a chance.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 4, 'Which list matches the settings the QI report states for every product?', '["Window, wavelet, weights and the geological chance of success.", "Iterations and seeds only, as the engines hold the window and the wavelet within them.", "Window, wavelet and its scale, LFM cut, weights, iterations and seeds.", "The recommendation and the support word, and nothing more."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The report states the method, the stated settings (window, wavelet and its scale, LFM cut, weights, iterations, seeds), the blind-well figures per parameter, the calibration and its left-out check, the prospect figures with their limits and every issue raised. QI never sets the chance, and no wavelet is estimated inside an inversion.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 5, 'A model-based inversion on the Ekene pack used a zero-phase Ricker of 25 Hz, half length 40 ms. Where did that wavelet come from?', '["modelBased estimated it from the trace in its 60 iterations.", "It is a stated input: no wavelet is estimated inside an inversion.", "The low-frequency model supplied it through its 8 Hz cut.", "simultaneousInversion estimated it inside its solve."]'::jsonb, 1, 'No wavelet is estimated inside an inversion; it is an input, on Ekene a zero-phase Ricker of 25 Hz, half length 40 ms, scaled at the wells. The LFM is a separate input with its 8 Hz cut.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 6, 'Where does the low-frequency model m0 of modelBased and simultaneousInversion come from?', '["modelBased builds it from the wells, low-passed at 8 Hz.", "simultaneousInversion builds one per parameter.", "Both read it from the seismic below 8 Hz.", "It is an input built beforehand; neither function builds an LFM."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No LFM is built inside modelBased or simultaneousInversion: m0 is an input, on Ekene built beforehand with an 8 Hz cut. The seismic band holds no trend below about 8 Hz, which is why the LFM comes from the wells.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 7, 'spillAnalysis runs on the Ekene Sand top grid of 25 by 20 nodes every 328.083990 ft. Where does that grid come from?', '["A stated input, the kit''s own grid of the six development wells'' tops; closure grids nothing.", "closure.js grids it from the four pack wells'' tops by inverse distance with a power of 2, the LFM''s rule.", "QI Studio picks the horizon on the full stack and closure.js grids the picks it is handed into nodes.", "The low-frequency model, whose horizons the closure engine reads back as the top grid."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Closure reads a stated grid: the kit grids the Ekene Sand top from the six development wells'' tops, 25 by 20 nodes, 201 mapped. The engines grid no surface and pick no horizon; inverse distance with power 2 belongs to the LFM between wells.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 8, 'A facies map calls a region oil sand. What may a report infer about its fluid from the class name?', '["Oil, since a class named for a fluid is a calibrated prediction measured at the wells.", "Oil at the share correct, 0.973443, read as the probability that each point holds oil.", "Nothing: no fluid is inferred from a class name, and oil sand is a fluid hypothesis.", "Oil, once the two-attribute posterior passes 0.997373 at the point being mapped."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engines infer no fluid from a class name. QI Studio labels a class named for a fluid fluid_hypothesis and every other class calibrated_prediction; 0.973443 is a share correct on the model''s own samples, and 0.997373 a posterior under stated priors.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 9, 'A porosity map from inverted AI (acoustic impedance) travels with which errors from the Ekene seismic-scale check?', '["s 0.017980 from the 644 log samples, the only scatter the band needs at seismic scale.", "s 0.007175 log against log, and the pooled left-out RMS 0.024072 that widened the band.", "The widening 0.022977 alone, since the log-against-log error is already carried inside it.", "s 0.007175 alone, since the left-out RMS describes the inversion and leaves the map."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At seismic scale s is 0.007175 log against log and the pooled left-out RMS is 0.024072, giving s_extra 0.022977. The report states the calibration and its left-out check; 0.017980 belongs to the log-scale fit.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 10, 'An AI volume from post-stack inversion travels with its blind-well figures. What blind correlations do the Ekene wells return?', '["From -0.345505 to -0.190352, the low-frequency model''s own correlations.", "From 0.758561 to 0.889063, the wells kept in their own LFM.", "From 0.796895 to 0.931237, coloured inversion against the band-passed log.", "From 0.759365 to 0.889320 across model-based and sparse-spike."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The inversions follow the shape, with blind correlations from 0.759365 to 0.889320 across both methods. The LFM alone correlates negatively at every well, and the kept-in and coloured figures are other checks.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 11, 'The Ekene Sand top grid spills at the map edge, 2.380249 ft below the crest. How does that column enter the report?', '["As the map''s limit, with the edge flag beside it.", "As the oil column of the trap.", "As the closure measured on the trap.", "Left out, as a refusal of the engine."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The report states the prospect figures with their limits. The spill is limited by the map edge, so the 2.380249 ft column is the map''s limit and no measurement of the trap; the engine still returns it with the flag.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 12, 'Why is the Ekene field never used as the Expert capstone case?', '["The pack''s logs are too short to invert.", "The Ekene grid spills at its edge, so the column could not be returned on it.", "The members-only videos speak the Ekene answers aloud.", "Its figures change with each engine run."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The capstone cases are never the Ekene field: the members-only videos speak the Ekene answers aloud, so a graded figure on Ekene could be answered by watching a video. The engines read no random number of their own.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 13, 'Which of these is one of the six figures the Expert capstone grades?', '["The recommendation the assessment returns for the case''s prospect.", "The column from crest to spill on the case''s depth grid, in feet.", "The facies class name most likely at a stated AI of the case.", "The geological chance of success the case''s figures imply."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The capstone grades six engine figures, the last the column from crest to spill on the case''s depth grid, in feet. Any verdict, class name or category is never graded, and QI never sets the chance.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 14, 'The capstone asks for the model-based AI at a stated sample, with the case''s supplied low-frequency model, wavelet, weight and iteration count. What does the learner build for the inversion?', '["Nothing: each of those is in the case file, as m0 and the wavelet are inputs to modelBased.", "The wavelet, extracted inside modelBased from the case''s target trace.", "The LFM, built inside modelBased from the case''s wells by inverse distance.", "The iteration count, chosen to give the smallest residual on the trace."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every input the six figures depend on is stated in the case file, and modelBased builds no LFM and estimates no wavelet. A smaller residual is no better inversion, and the count is the case''s.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-report-and-the-limits', 15, 'The net sand cut-off on the Ekene pack is 0.350000. Where do the engines get it?', '["From the kit''s VSH log, which the engines read from the pack''s files.", "From a value the engines carry within them for every field that the caller does not name.", "From the facies model, which sets the cut-off at the boundary where its shale prior falls.", "From the caller: the engines hold no Ekene figure, and the cut-off is a stated input."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engines hold no Ekene figure: every cut-off, window, angle and contact is an input the caller states, and no engine reads a file. The 0.350000 cut-off is a selection the course states before any function is called.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Before any inversion is trusted, the operator d = C D m is checked step by step on two seeded vectors. Which figures show that both steps pass?', '["D prints -0.753789 forward and 38.991017 backward, the two steps sharing one test of the whole operator end to end.", "D prints -0.753789 on both sides and C prints 38.991017 on both sides, each with a difference of 0.000000.", "Only C is tested, at 38.991017, a centred first difference being its own transpose and needing no check.", "C prints 344.213395 and 341.516846, the synthetic and the trace at 1288.000000 ms agreeing closely."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course tests each step: the centred difference D gives -0.753789 for (A x) . y and for x . (A^T y), the convolution C gives 38.991017 for both, and each difference is 0.000000. 344.213395 and 341.516846 are the Ekene-1 synthetic and trace at 1288.000000 ms, no part of the adjoint test.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Why does the forward model take m = ln(AI), the natural log of acoustic impedance, as its unknown?', '["The log carries the trend below about 8 Hz, so the seismic band supplies the level and no low-frequency model is needed.", "Taking the log removes the wavelet, so the trace is d = D m with no convolution.", "The log makes each reflection the full change of ln(AI) across two samples, with no factor of one half anywhere.", "Half the difference of ln(AI) is close to the reflection coefficient for small contrasts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course writes the model in ln(AI) because half the difference of ln(AI) is close to the reflection coefficient for small contrasts; the trace is d = C D m with D the centred difference, half the change across two samples. The seismic band holds no trend, so the LFM supplies what lies below about 8 Hz.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'Ekene-1 is left out of its own low-frequency model. Which of the remaining wells takes the largest inverse-distance share at Ekene-1, and how is a share formed?', '["Ekene-2, 0.477419 at 1209.338662 m; each weight is the inverse square of the distance, and a share is that weight over the sum of the three.", "Ekene-4, 0.477419 at 2193.171220 m, the farthest well weighted most so the model reaches across the field.", "Ekene-3, 0.377419 at 1360.147051 m, each share being the plain inverse of the distance over the sum.", "Each of the three wells an equal share, the power of 2 acting only once the horizons are mapped."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course prints 0.477419 for Ekene-2, the well 1209.338662 m away, with weights falling as the square of distance grows. Ekene-3''s share is 0.377419, and equal shares would ignore the power of 2 the course states.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Ekene-1''s sand top at 1288.796378 ms maps to Ekene-2''s top at 1305.746335 ms. Above that horizon, Ekene-1''s 1280.796378 ms maps to 1297.746335 ms. Which rule of mapToWellTime places it?', '["The proportional position between top and base is kept, as it is for every sample inside the sand.", "The sample takes the nearest covered value, as an uncovered one does.", "Above the first horizon the time offset from that horizon is kept.", "The two-way time is carried across unchanged, the LFM holding one clock at every well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Above the first horizon and below the last, mapToWellTime keeps the time offset from that horizon, which is why 1280.796378 ms lands at 1297.746335 ms. The proportional position applies between two horizons, and the nearest covered value fills samples no well covers.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Model-based inversion needs the 25 Hz Ricker scaled at the wells before it runs. What makes that scale necessary?', '["The scale supplies the level of AI that the band-limited trace lacks below about 8 Hz.", "A wavelet chosen for its shape carries an arbitrary amplitude, and the inversion reads impedance contrast from the ratio of the trace to the synthetic.", "The scale reshapes the Ricker until its spectrum matches the full stack at every well.", "The scale converts the trace from one-way to two-way time before the convolution."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The scale is one number, 769.984107 over 19 samples on the pack, that makes s times the forward model match the seismic; it is needed because the shape carries an arbitrary amplitude and the inversion reads contrast from the trace over the synthetic. The level below about 8 Hz comes from the LFM, and one number cannot change the wavelet''s shape.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'The stated Ricker spans 21 samples. When the well-wavelet extraction under QI Studio''s angle-wavelet engine is asked for an 11-sample wavelet on Ekene-1''s short log, which message comes back?', '["\"The well reflectivity is flat over the seismic at the well, so there is nothing to extract a wavelet from.\"", "\"The wells overlap too little seismic to scale the wavelet.\"", "\"The log covers 16 usable samples at the well; a 11-sample wavelet needs at least 22. Shorten the wavelet or use the statistical one.\"", "None: it returns an 11-sample wavelet for each stack, the 27 logged samples clearing the need."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a half length of 5 the engine gives the message in the key, in its own words. The flat-reflectivity message comes at a half length of 15, the overlap message is waveletScale''s, and a wavelet per stack comes back at a half length of 3, 7 samples long.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Which stated frequency is both the high cut of the log an inversion is scored against and the low-pass calibrateProperty applies to each well''s ln(AI) and porosity at seismic scale?', '["8 Hz, the low-frequency cut of the LFM and the crossover sparse-spike takes its low frequencies through.", "60 Hz, the upper edge of the coloured band from 8 to 60 Hz.", "25 Hz, the frequency of the stated zero-phase Ricker wavelet.", "50 Hz in both places."]'::jsonb, 3, 'One figure does both jobs: inversions are scored on the log high-cut at 50 Hz, and calibrateProperty low-passes ln(AI) and porosity at 50 Hz before it fits. The LFM and the sparse-spike crossover sit at 8 Hz, the coloured band tops out at 60 Hz, and the Ricker is a 25 Hz wavelet.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'A report states the seeds behind every figure. Which seeds and noise level produce the mean relative spread in AI of 0.645787 at Ekene-1?', '["The 20 seeds 101 to 120, with noise at a signal-to-noise ratio of 4.", "Seed 42 of mulberry32, the dot-product test''s seed.", "The same 20 seeds, 101 to 120, with the added noise at a signal-to-noise ratio of 8.", "None: the engine draws its noise from the clock."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The realisations add seeded Gaussian noise at a signal-to-noise ratio of 4 on the 20 seeds 101 to 120, giving a mean relative spread of 0.645787 over the 27 logged samples. Seed 42 drives the adjoint test, and no engine reads a clock or a random number of its own.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'The full-stack wavelet scale is 769.984107. Which statement about the three per-stack scales the course measures by the same pooled least-squares rule is printed?', '["The near-stack scale equals the full-stack scale, 769.984107, the near angle of 10 degrees standing in for zero offset.", "The far-stack scale, 1305.654435, is the largest, a far stack carrying more high frequency than a near one.", "Each comes from extractAngleWavelets at a half length of 15 samples, one 31-sample wavelet per stack.", "Each rests on 19 samples, the count behind the full-stack scale."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Near 1860.598608, mid 1679.662569 and far 1305.654435 each rest on 19 samples, as the full-stack scale of 769.984107 does. The near scale is the largest, a far stack carries less high frequency, and at a half length of 15 the engine returns no wavelet for any stack.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'With the iteration count held at 300, a heavier weight eps of 1.000000 is tried in the Ekene-1 blind run. What scores come back?', '["0.732457 for the correlation and 13.421322 percent for the error, the scores of eps 5.000000.", "0.091593 and 52.851805 percent, eps having no effect once 300 iterations run.", "0.219646 for the correlation, 33.801729 percent for the error, 1.320986 for the residual.", "0.583443 and 19.965981 percent, the 120-iteration row at eps 0.050000."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Raising eps to 1.000000 lifts the correlation to 0.219646 and cuts the error to 33.801729 percent, the residual reading 1.320986. The pair 0.091593 and 52.851805 is the lighter weight, 0.732457 and 13.421322 the weight of 5.000000, and 0.583443 and 19.965981 come from 120 iterations.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'On the 101-sample Ekene-1 trace, sparse-spike is run at lambda 1000.000000. How many reflectivity samples stay non-zero at 60 and at 400 iterations?', '["101 at both counts, as at QI Studio''s starting lambda of 0.002000.", "67 at 60 iterations, falling to 44 at 400.", "36 at 60 and 27 at 400, the counts at lambda 5000.000000.", "96 and 61, the lambda 200.000000 counts."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At lambda 1000.000000 the course prints 67 non-zero samples at 60 iterations and 44 at 400; more iterations sharpen the sparsity. 101 is the count at 0.002000, 96 and 61 belong to 200.000000, and 36 and 27 to 5000.000000.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'Coloured inversion at Ekene-1 correlates 0.931237 with the log band-passed between 8 and 50 Hz. Why does the course score it by correlation alone?', '["Its output is relative impedance, band-limited with no level, so there is no level to compare.", "Its operator carries the LFM''s level from the wells, and an RMS error would count that level twice over.", "Its wavelet scale of 769.984107 leaves every AI error in trace units.", "Its slope alpha of -1.012769 is fitted to the trace, fixing the error at zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Coloured inversion uses no wavelet and no LFM: one operator with phase minus 90 degrees and slope alpha -1.012769 fitted to the wells'' logs, so its output is relative impedance with no level, scored by correlation alone. The scale 769.984107 belongs to the wavelet the absolute inversions use.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'sparseSpike solves for a sparse reflectivity r by FISTA. How does it reach an absolute ln(AI) from r?', '["It integrates r once from the top sample, taking its level from the first logged AI at the well.", "It runs CGLS on r with eps 0.050000 towards the LFM, as modelBased does with m.", "It multiplies r by the wavelet scale of 769.984107, which carries the level of AI.", "It integrates r to a relative ln(AI), m_(i+1) = m_(i-1) + 2 r_i, and takes the low frequencies from the LFM through a moving-average crossover at 8 Hz."]'::jsonb, 3, 'sparseSpike integrates r into a relative ln(AI) by m_(i+1) = m_(i-1) + 2 r_i, levels each of the two interleaved chains to its mean, and takes the low frequencies from the LFM through an 8 Hz moving-average crossover. CGLS towards m0 is modelBased''s solver, and the scale 769.984107 only joins wavelet and trace.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'At Ekene-4, blind, which three AI correlations do model-based post-stack, sparse-spike and simultaneous inversion from near, mid and far return, in that order?', '["0.852881, 0.873414 and 0.863789, the Ekene-1 figures.", "0.759365, 0.778867 and 0.780557.", "0.889320, 0.881157 and 0.854299, the Ekene-2 figures.", "0.847503, 0.821455 and 0.847660, the Ekene-3 figures."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At Ekene-4 the blind AI correlations are 0.759365 for model-based, 0.778867 for sparse-spike and 0.780557 for the simultaneous inversion from all three stacks. The other options are the same three figures at Ekene-1, Ekene-2 and Ekene-3.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'Over 20 realisations at Ekene-1, the 1304.000000 ms row reads Q10 5069751.189881, Q50 6629158.255936 and Q90 9889658.945117. What does relativeSpread return there, and which of the three is the low figure?', '["0.727077, with Q90 the low figure as for a volume.", "0.645787, the mean spread over the 27 logged samples, with Q10 as the low figure.", "0.727077, from (Q90 - Q10) / Q50, Q10 being the low end.", "0.498207, the spread one row up at 1288.000000 ms, again with Q10 as the low figure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'relativeSpread divides Q90 - Q10 by Q50, giving 0.727077 at 1304.000000 ms. For a parameter Q10 is the 10th percentile and the low end; the convention with P90 low belongs to outcomes such as volumes. 0.645787 is the mean over 27 samples and 0.498207 the 1288.000000 ms row.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'Fatti''s linearisation writes R(theta) = G1 dln(AI) + G2 dln(SI) + G3 dln(rho), with K the background Vs/Vp. Which coefficient does K not enter?', '["G1, written (1 + tan^2 theta) / 2.", "G2, written -4 K^2 sin^2 theta, the shear term K scales.", "G3, written (4 K^2 sin^2 theta - tan^2 theta) / 2.", "None of the three: K enters each one through tan^2 theta."]'::jsonb, 0, 'G1 = (1 + tan^2 theta) / 2 holds no K, while G2 = -4 K^2 sin^2 theta and G3 = (4 K^2 sin^2 theta - tan^2 theta) / 2 both carry K^2. On the pack K is 0.394396, the mean Vs / Vp over the logged samples in the window.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'At Ekene-3, blind from the near, mid and far stacks, what does the simultaneous inversion score for density?', '["A correlation of 0.360220 and an error of 12.979404 percent, density following Vp/Vs at this well.", "A correlation of 0.847660 and an error of 10.653026 percent, density riding on the shape of AI.", "-0.413424 and 9.115991 percent, Ekene-4''s figures.", "A correlation of -0.719301 and an error of 16.816175 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-3''s density row reads -0.719301 and 16.816175 percent. 0.360220 and 12.979404 are its Vp/Vs figures, 0.847660 and 10.653026 its AI figures, and -0.413424 with 9.115991 is Ekene-4''s density.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'Post-stack model-based inversion at Ekene-1, blind, scores an AI error of 10.557858 percent. What AI error and correlation does the simultaneous inversion from near, mid and far return at the same well, blind?', '["10.557858 percent again, the simultaneous run reproducing the post-stack AI exactly.", "8.956955 percent, with an AI correlation of 0.863789.", "9.807415 percent with 0.858677, near stack only.", "8.638086 percent with -0.190352, the three LFMs'' figures with no seismic."]'::jsonb, 1, 'From near, mid and far the simultaneous inversion at Ekene-1 gives AI 0.863789 and 8.956955 percent. 9.807415 and 0.858677 come from the near stack alone, and -0.190352 with 8.638086 from the three LFMs with no seismic.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'At the far stack''s angle of 30 degrees and K 0.394396, what does Fatti''s SI coefficient G2 print beside G1?', '["G2 -0.072783 beside G1 0.566237, the 20 degree pair.", "G2 -0.257075 beside G1 0.852044, the 40 degree pair.", "G2 -0.155548 beside G1 0.666667.", "G2 -0.088893 beside G1 0.666667, the density value read as shear."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 30 degrees the coefficients are G1 0.666667, G2 -0.155548 and G3 -0.088893. The pairs at 20 and 40 degrees are -0.072783 with 0.566237 and -0.257075 with 0.852044.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'Ekene-1 is inverted with its own log left out of the LFMs, from two stacks only, near and mid. Which scores do Vp/Vs and density print?', '["Vp/Vs 0.383124 with 13.660754 percent; density -0.707948 with 12.184029 percent.", "Vp/Vs -0.744719 with 30.824755 percent; density 0.614032 with 0.627310 percent, the near-only row.", "Vp/Vs 0.470296 with 10.707173 percent; density -0.767957 with 14.112317 percent, all three stacks.", "Vp/Vs 0.048283 with 11.122749 percent; density -0.087754 with 0.767482 percent, the LFMs alone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With near and mid the course prints Vp/Vs 0.383124 and 13.660754 percent and density -0.707948 and 12.184029 percent. The other rows are the near stack alone, all three stacks and the LFMs with no seismic.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'The two-attribute facies model on AI and Vp/Vs scores a share correct of 0.973443 on its own samples at log scale. At which pack wells is the blind Vp/Vs correlation of the simultaneous inversion negative?', '["Ekene-1 and Ekene-3, at 0.470296 and 0.360220, the wells nearest the oil leg.", "All four, from -0.767957 to -0.413424, the inversion running against the log.", "None: Vp/Vs follows the log at every well, as AI does from 0.780557 up.", "Ekene-2 and Ekene-4, at -0.287516 and -0.132709."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Blind, from near, mid and far, Vp/Vs correlates -0.287516 at Ekene-2 and -0.132709 at Ekene-4, and positively at Ekene-1 (0.470296) and Ekene-3 (0.360220). -0.767957 to -0.413424 is density''s range, so a facies map on inverted Vp/Vs rests on a parameter the blind wells do not support.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'At AI 6500000.000000, what porosity and 80 percent band does the log-scale transform return?', '["0.203850, between 0.180764 and 0.226936, the figures at AI 7000000.000000.", "0.215267, between 0.192157 and 0.238378.", "0.192433 between 0.169342 and 0.215525, read at AI 7500000.000000.", "0.215267 between 0.174207 and 0.233493, a band from the 90 percent level."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 6500000.000000 the transform predicts 0.215267 with Q10 0.192157 and Q90 0.238378 at the 80 percent level; the slope is negative, so softer rock reads more porous. 0.174207 to 0.233493 is the 90 percent interval at AI 7000000.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Refitted at seismic scale on each well''s porosity inside its Ekene Sand times, what transform does calibrateProperty fit?', '["Intercept 0.363690 and slope -0.022834 on 644 samples, the log-scale fit carried up unchanged.", "Intercept 0.302982 and slope -0.017033 on 644 samples, the log samples kept at full count.", "s 0.007175 and RMS 0.024072, no line refitted.", "Intercept 0.302982 and slope -0.017033 on 20 upscaled samples."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fitted on the upscaled samples inside the sand times, the seismic-scale line has intercept 0.302982 and slope -0.017033 on n 20. The 644-sample line at log scale is a separate fit, and 0.007175 and 0.024072 are the two errors that travel beside the refitted line.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Left out of the seismic-scale porosity check, Ekene-2 is predicted from its blind inverted AI on 6 samples. What correlation and band coverage does it return?', '["Correlation NaN, its predicted series showing no spread over the samples, with 1.000000 inside the band.", "Correlation -0.484704, with 0.600000 inside the band, as at Ekene-1.", "Correlation -0.794283, with 0.833333 of its samples inside the widened 80 percent band.", "Correlation -0.734150 and 1.000000 inside, the Ekene-3 pair."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-2 left out returns a correlation of -0.794283, an RMS error of 0.027716 and a share inside the widened band of 0.833333. The NaN belongs to Ekene-4, -0.484704 with 0.600000 to Ekene-1, and -0.734150 with 1.000000 to Ekene-3.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'On AI alone at 6600000.000000, the sample-proportion priors make brine sand most likely at 0.557911. What do equal priors return at the same AI?', '["Oil sand at 0.673039 and brine sand at 0.326961: the prior is a stated choice, and it moves the answer.", "Brine sand still at 0.557911, a prior being unable to move a posterior where shale reads 0.000000.", "Shale at 0.796396, equal priors handing it the share its 2519 samples carry.", "Oil sand at 0.997373, the call the two-attribute model makes at Vp/Vs 1.750000."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With equal priors at 6600000.000000 the posteriors are shale 0.000000, oil sand 0.673039 and brine sand 0.326961, so the call flips. 0.796396 is shale''s sample-proportion prior, and 0.997373 needs Vp/Vs 1.750000 as a second attribute.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'With Vp/Vs added to AI, how does the two-attribute confusion matrix call the 465 brine-sand samples?', '["439 brine sand and 26 oil sand, the counts of the model on AI alone.", "455 brine sand, 9 oil sand and 1 shale.", "455 brine sand and 10 oil sand, every miss going to the oil sand.", "160 brine sand and 19 oil sand, the oil-sand row read across."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two-attribute brine-sand row reads 1 shale, 9 oil sand and 455 brine sand, up from 439 on AI alone, where 26 went to oil sand. 160 and 19 are the two-attribute oil-sand row.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'How does QI Studio label a facies class named for a fluid, such as oil sand or brine sand?', '["calibrated_prediction, like every class.", "fluid_measurement, for a class scoring above 0.934872.", "oil_sand or brine_sand, the fluid read straight from the class name and carried through to every map.", "fluid_hypothesis, and every other class calibrated_prediction."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pore fluid behind a fluid-named class is an interpretation, so its label is fluid_hypothesis; shale carries calibrated_prediction. No engine reads a fluid from a class name, whatever share correct, 0.934872 or 0.973443, the model scores.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'calibrateProperty leaves each well out, refits the porosity transform on the others, and predicts that well''s porosity. From which AI does it predict?', '["The LFM alone at that well, whose AI error at Ekene-1 left out is 8.638086 percent.", "The well''s own log AI after the 50 Hz high cut, so the residuals match s of 0.007175.", "The blind inverted AI at that well, from the inversion with the well left out of its LFM.", "Coloured inversion''s relative impedance, the one product built with no LFM between wells, so no level leaks in."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The check predicts each left-out well from the AI the blind inversion returns there, its LFM built from the other wells; that is why the pooled left-out RMS, 0.024072, exceeds the log-against-log s of 0.007175. Coloured inversion has no level to feed a transform.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'The closure engine reads the Ekene Sand top grid of 25 by 20 nodes every 328.083990 ft. In what units does it read elevation and spacing?', '["In one unit for both, which the course runs in feet, with z minus the depth below the kelly bushing.", "Elevation in feet and spacing in metres, the kit''s projection unit, converted inside the engine.", "Both in metres, the unit the kit writes, the course converting only the printed column.", "Elevation as two-way time in milliseconds, the spacing in feet."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine reads elevation and spacing in one unit, and the course runs it in feet; z is minus the depth below the kelly bushing, so the crest reads -5051.568570 ft and the wells'' contact -5118.110236 ft.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Designed teaching anomaly A, every mapped node shallower than the spill, has an edge scatter of 0.643935 ft. What conformance and implied contact does anomalyConformance return?', '["Conformance 1.000000, its share inside the closure, with the contact sitting exactly at the spill of -5053.948819 ft.", "Conformance 0.729467, implied contact -5053.959008 ft.", "Conformance 0.000000, at -5068.442175 ft.", "Conformance 0.643935, the scatter read directly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Conformance is 1 - scatter / relief, and for A it is 0.729467 with an implied contact of -5053.959008 ft, just below the spill at -5053.948819 ft. 1.000000 is A''s share inside the closure, and 0.000000 with -5068.442175 ft is anomaly B.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Designed teaching anomaly B keeps every node shallower than a level 20 ft below the spill, west of the grid''s middle column only. What node counts and share inside the closure does the engine return?', '["11 nodes, 9 of them edge nodes, wholly inside at 1.000000, the counts of anomaly A.", "201 nodes, every mapped node of the grid, with 0.152778 inside.", "72 nodes, every one on the edge, the straight cut adding edge all along, 1.000000 inside.", "72 nodes, 22 of them edge nodes, with 0.152778 inside the closure."]'::jsonb, 3, 'B holds 72 nodes with 22 edge nodes, and 0.152778 of it lies inside the closure; its conformance clips to 0.000000. 11 nodes with 9 edge nodes is anomaly A, wholly inside, and 201 is the count of mapped nodes on the grid.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'No anomaly is seen on a prospect. Which stated input decides between downgrade and retain?', '["The count of independent sources: downgrade with one, retain with two.", "The conformance: downgrade under 0.3, retain at 0.7 and above.", "The feasibility verdict: downgrade with against where it is feasible, retain with neutral where it is not stated.", "A competing explanation: downgrade where it is ruled out, retain where it is open."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Feasibility decides what absence means. With the case visible, a missing anomaly downgrades the prospect and the support reads against; with feasibility unstated the prospect is retained and the support is neutral. Source counts and the 0.3 and 0.7 thresholds act on an anomaly that is present.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Which two cases in the assessment''s decision table return a seismic support of neutral?', '["No anomaly with feasibility not stated, and an anomaly present with a competing explanation judged likely.", "No anomaly on feasible data, and an anomaly with one independent source.", "An anomaly with conformance 0.83, and one with 0.9 inside the closure.", "Every case recommending investigate, the word neutral following the recommendation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'neutral comes from the retain row (no anomaly, feasibility not stated) and the competing-explanation row. No anomaly on feasible data reads against, and one independent source returns investigate with supports, so support and recommendation move apart.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'spillAnalysis floods outward from the crest, and climbToCrest climbs from a picked point. How many neighbours does each consider at a node?', '["Eight for both, so a diagonal saddle counts for the spill as it does for the crest.", "Four for the flood, which uses four-neighbour connectivity, and eight for the climb.", "Four for both, the climb stepping only along rows and columns.", "Eight for the flood and four for the climb, the flood needing diagonals to reach the edge."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The flood that finds the spill uses four-neighbour connectivity; the climb steps to the highest of its eight neighbours. On the Ekene grid the climb from column 12, row 9 reaches node 310 at -5051.568570 ft.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'An interpreter judges likely the competing explanation that a soft impedance anomaly is brine sand, citing the AI-only confusion matrix where 127 of the 179 oil-sand samples are called brine sand. The anomaly is present, every other input as in the decision table''s first row (conformance 0.83, two independent sources, feasible). What does the assessment return?', '["mature, supports, the anomaly being present with two independent sources behind it.", "downgrade, against, the rival explanation counting as the absence of an anomaly.", "retain, neutral, the row for no anomaly with feasibility unstated.", "investigate, the likely rival holding the seismic support at neutral."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A competing explanation judged likely holds the prospect at investigate with neutral support, the table''s last row. On AI alone 127 of the 179 oil-sand samples are called brine sand, which is why brine sand is a real rival; downgrade and retain are the two rows with no anomaly.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Only two attributes back a soft anomaly: the RMS amplitude of the full stack, and AI inverted from that same full stack. What does the evidence count make of them, and what would maturing need?', '["Two, the inversion adding the LFM''s information from the wells to the full stack, which is enough to mature.", "Two, one per attribute, though maturing needs three.", "One, the shared source full_stack; maturing needs two independent supporting sources.", "None, an impedance being no supporting item, so the prospect is downgraded."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two attributes drawn from one seismic response count once, under the source full_stack. Maturing takes two independent supporting sources, which the course''s worked case gets from a far-stack brightening, a second response.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'QI never sets a prospect''s geological chance of success. Who weighs what a QI study reports, and what does it report?', '["The interpreter alone, who turns the oil-sand posterior of 0.997373 at Vp/Vs 1.750000 into the chance.", "The risk team weighs it; QI reports what the seismic says, supports, neutral or against, with a recommendation.", "The closure engine, which weighs the gross rock volume of 1519731.367693 ft3 at the spill.", "The assessment engine, which reports a probability of success beside its recommendation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'QI reports the seismic support for the risk team to weigh, and prospectAssessment.js returns a recommendation and a seismic support with no probability of success. A posterior such as 0.997373 is a probability under the facies model, and closure returns rock volume only.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Which of these does no engine in the Expert tier compute?', '["A picked horizon.", "The blind-well score, the correlation and RMS error at a left-out well.", "The quantiles across realisations, Q10, Q50 and Q90 per sample.", "The gross rock volume at the spill of a stated grid."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engines pick no horizon. lfm.js computes the blind-well score, inversionSpread.js the quantiles across realisations, and closure the gross rock volume, 1519731.367693 ft3 at the Ekene spill.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'The Expert capstone asks for six figures read from the inversion and prospect explorers. On what data does it run?', '["The Ekene pack, read at Ekene-1 on the tier''s settings.", "The Ekene pack with fresh noise seeds, so its figures differ from those of the 20 seeds 101 to 120.", "A case built from the members-only videos.", "A held-out synthetic case supplied as a case file; the Ekene field is never used."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The capstone runs on a held-out synthetic case the learner pastes into the panels. The Ekene field is never used because the members-only videos speak the Ekene answers aloud, and no Ekene figure is graded, the 20 seeds 101 to 120 included.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Beyond the products themselves, what goes into the handover record of a QI study?', '["The products alone, each volume carrying its own settings, its 8 Hz LFM cut and its 50 Hz high cut in its header.", "The inverted volumes and the recommendation, the settings left at QI Studio''s starting values such as eps 0.050000.", "The method, the stated settings, the blind-well figures per parameter, the calibration and its left-out check, the prospect figures with their limits, and every issue raised.", "The method and the geological chance of success, the two figures a risk team reads first."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The handover files are the products together with the record of how each was made; a product without it cannot be checked. An 8 Hz cut or an eps of 0.050000 is a setting that record names, and the chance of success stays with the risk team.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'Which reference does the course name for the sparse reflectivity that sparse-spike inversion solves by FISTA?', '["Lancaster and Whitcombe (2000), SEG Expanded Abstracts.", "Beck and Teboulle (2009), SIAM Journal on Imaging Sciences 2.", "Hyndman and Fan (1996), type 7.", "Fatti and others (1994), Geophysics 59, the reference for the linearisation in three parameters."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course names Beck and Teboulle (2009) for sparse reflectivity by FISTA. Lancaster and Whitcombe (2000) is coloured inversion, Hyndman and Fan (1996) type 7 the quantiles, and Fatti and others (1994) the simultaneous inversion.'),
  ('qi', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'The closure at the spill on the Ekene grid returns a gross rock volume of 1519731.367693 ft3. What does the closure engine return in barrels?', '["Nothing: closure returns gross rock volume only, and no volume in barrels.", "1519731.367693 barrels, the engine reading the grid''s cubic feet as barrels of rock.", "The oil in place over the 11 nodes, from the transform''s porosity of 0.215267 at AI 6500000.000000.", "A volume in barrels at the contact, -5118.110236 ft."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engines compute no volume in barrels, so 1519731.367693 ft3 stays a rock volume. At the wells'' contact of -5118.110236 ft the closure is reported open, and closure returns gross rock volume only.');
